import json,os
os.makedirs('models',exist_ok=True)
poly=json.load(open('final_refit_results.json'))[0]
N={q['label']:q for q in json.load(open('nonlinear_dynamic_results.json'))}
ref='Reference for polynomial right-hand-side libraries: Brunton, Proctor and Kutz, Discovering governing equations from data by sparse identification of nonlinear dynamical systems, arXiv:1509.03580 (2015), related journal DOI 10.1073/pnas.1517384113 (title, authors and DOI checked on the official arXiv abstract page). This run used dense ordinary least squares and manually restricted structures, not the SINDy sparse algorithm.'
methodref='The optimizer is scipy.optimize.least_squares, default Trust Region Reflective algorithm, finite-difference two-point Jacobian and linear loss; its official documentation was checked online. Nonlinear least squares was used because RK4 output error is nonlinear even in coefficients of a polynomial vector field.'
data='Both complete files data/train.csv (one stated trajectory) and data/val.csv (one stated trajectory) were used in the final fit. Training-only fits were compared on validation first. During final fitting each file was initialized only at its first observed state, with no later state resets; all subsequent observations contributed equal output-error weight. Sampling period was 5 s, with current-row inputs held constant during each classical RK4 step. Validation was subsequently used for fitting, so final validation errors are in-sample and are not unbiased generalization estimates.'
criterion='Final criterion: sum of squared free-running errors in `y` on both available trajectories, excluding only the first state of each file, to match the evaluation criterion. Initial derivative least squares and then teacher-forced one-step RK4 output least squares supplied starting values; those criteria do not define the final estimates.'
limit='No system description or units are supplied, so these are empirical vector fields rather than asserted physical laws. Unit labels state-unit, x1-unit and x2-unit denote unknown logged units; only seconds are known. The fitted domain is approximately `0.99 <= y <= 2.19` state-unit, `1.63 <= x1 <= 94.45` x1-unit, and `0.08 <= x2 <= 30.80` x2-unit on the two supplied files. Polynomial derivative residuals have temporal dependence and correlate with the next change in `x2`; this suggests sampling alignment or omitted dynamics, but is not proof of a particular mechanism. Abrupt near-baseline states midway through the files are not reset in fitting or prediction. No memory, next input, or hidden state is included in the delivered expressions; the irreducible consequences of these omissions remain. Extrapolation and input regimes not represented here are unvalidated.'
def par(n,v,unit,typ,desc):return dict(name=n,value=float(v),unit=unit,type=typ,description=desc)
def common():return [par('n1',70,'x1-unit','fixed','Convenient input normalization, selected by inspection of the training input scale, not optimized; gives dimensionless `x1/n1`.'),par('n2',20,'x2-unit','fixed','Convenient input normalization selected by inspection of the training input scale, not optimized; gives dimensionless `x2/n2`.')]
def scaled():return common()+[par('yc',1.3,'state-unit','fixed','Convenient state center near the training mean, selected rather than optimized.'),par('ys',.3,'state-unit','fixed','Convenient state scaling of the order of the training standard deviation, selected rather than optimized; `(y-yc)/ys` is dimensionless.')]
def monom(power,z='((y-yc)/ys)'):
 factors=[]
 for s,k in zip([z,'(x1/n1)','(x2/n2)'],power):
  if k: factors.append(s if k==1 else s+'**'+str(k))
 return '*'.join(factors) if factors else '1'
def model(label,expr,params,method,script,family,interpret,origin,limitations):
 doc=dict(expressions={'dy':expr},parameters=params,estimation={'criterion':criterion,'data':data,'method':method,'script':script},discussion={'family':family,'interpretation':interpret,'origin':origin,'limitations':limitations});json.dump(doc,open('models/'+label+'.json','w'),indent=2)
# Polynomial
powers=poly['powers'];co=poly['coefficients'];expr=' + '.join('c'+str(i)+'*'+monom(p) for i,p in enumerate(powers));pa=scaled()+[par('c'+str(i),v,'state-unit/s','fitted','Coefficient multiplying `'+monom(p)+'`; estimated jointly by the sequence described in estimation.') for i,(v,p) in enumerate(zip(co,powers))]
method='Derivative initialization: numpy.linalg.lstsq on all training rows, no starting values needed because this stage is linear. All coefficients then jointly minimized teacher-forced one-step RK4 error on training, followed by full training simulation error, using '+methodref+' Training stages used max_nfev 100 and 180 respectively, with convergence tolerances 1e-10 and 1e-9. Final all-data fit used max_nfev 180 and all tolerances 1e-10. Its numeric starting coefficient vector in parameter order was '+str(poly['training_only'])+' state-unit/s. The all-data fit is reproduced by final_refit.py using those stored training-only initial values; fit_dynamic.py reproduces earlier training stages. No randomized initialization or seed was used.'
model('CubicPolynomial',expr,pa,method,'final_refit.py','Continuous-time cubic polynomial state-space vector field. '+ref,'`y` is the state, `x1` and `x2` are inputs and `dy` is its time derivative. The constant is drift; state-only terms provide nonlinear restoring or amplifying effects, input-only terms provide nonlinear forcing, and mixed products alter the state dependence with the inputs. Every monomial and its coefficient are explicitly listed in parameters; no physical role is asserted.','An affine baseline was inadequate. Complete total-degree polynomial libraries of degrees one through five were screened by derivative error and free-running validation. Cubic provided a good tradeoff: higher degrees did not improve validation consistently and sometimes caused divergent simulation. All terms up to total degree three were retained as a transparent generic interaction library, rather than selecting many highly correlated individual terms.',limit+' Cubic terms can behave very differently outside the fitted box; stability is not guaranteed by the fit.')
# Rational
q=N['rational_both'];p=q['parameters'];powers=next(a for a in json.load(open('poly_results.json')) if a['kind']=='raw' and a['degree']==2)['powers'];num=' + '.join('c'+str(i)+'*'+monom(po) for i,po in enumerate(powers));expr='('+num+')/(1+d*((y-yc)/ys)**2)';pa=scaled()+[par('c'+str(i),p[i],'state-unit/s','fitted','Coefficient multiplying `'+monom(po)+'` in the numerator; fitted jointly.') for i,po in enumerate(powers)]+[par('d',p[10],'-','fitted','Nonnegative state-dependent denominator coefficient; reduces vector-field magnitude away from the state center.')]
# Reused nonlinear stage documentation

def nm(q):
 return 'All parameters were estimated jointly at every stage. Initial derivative fit on all training rows used '+methodref+' Numeric initialization in the expression parameter order was '+str(q['initial'])+'; units are those of the corresponding parameter rows. This seed came from the previously fitted helper model or polynomial model, except exponential and sigmoid seeds, which were moderate hand-chosen coefficients and scale parameters. Numeric derivative-stage output was '+str(q['derivative'])+' and numeric one-step-stage output was '+str(q['onestep'])+'. These numbers are in the corresponding parameter units. They document actual starts, not extra constants in the model. Bounds in the same order were '+str(q['lo'])+' to '+str(q['hi'])+', in the corresponding parameter units; these prevent extreme coefficients and invalid powers while permitting broad empirical nonlinear shapes. Max_nfev was 300 for derivative initialization, 180 for one-step training fit, and 200 for free-running fit; default convergence tolerances were 1e-8. The final fit on both files started directly from the documented one-step values, not from the training-only output-error fit. A separate training-only free-running fit was used for validation comparison. No random numbers or seed were used. nonlinear_dynamic.py reproduces the sequence and retains the exact original initialization vectors in its referenced working-directory result files.'
model('RationalGain',expr,pa,nm(q),'nonlinear_dynamic.py','Rational nonlinear state-space vector field with a polynomial numerator and a positive quadratic denominator. The polynomial-library context is '+ref,'Numerator monomials represent drift, nonlinear state feedback, nonlinear input forcing and mixed interactions. The positive term `d*((y-yc)/ys)**2` attenuates all these terms as the state departs from the center; `d` is dimensionless and constrained nonnegative. This is an empirical state-dependent time scale, not an identified storage law.','Added a state-dependent denominator after quadratic polynomial fits left state-dependent residual patterns. A denominator of `1+d*((y-yc)/ys)**2` was selected to avoid singularities while allowing state-dependent gain. Its numerator is the complete quadratic library, not an inferred physical equation.',limit+' Denominator positivity prevents poles but does not enforce dissipativity or extrapolation accuracy.')
# power/exponential/saturating nonlinear feedback
for label,key,mode in [('PowerFeedback','power_cross_both','power'),('ExponentialFeedback','exp_feedback_both','exp'),('SaturatingFeedback','sigmoid_feedback_both','sigmoid')]:
 q=N[key];p=q['parameters'];pa=common();floor=par('epsy',1e-6,'state-unit','fixed','Small positive floor selected for the shifted state before fractional powers or square roots; ensures real finite evaluation near and below the offset, not inferred as a physical threshold.')
 if mode=='power':
  z='(Max(y-h,epsy)**b)';v='(Max(x1/n1,epsu)**a*Max(x2/n2,epsu)**d)';g='(Max(x2/n2,epsu)**e)';expr=f'c0 + c1*{z} + c2*{v} + c3*{g} + c4*{v}*{z} + c5*{g}*{z}'
  pa += [par('c'+str(i),p[i],['state-unit/s','state-unit**(1-b)/s','state-unit/s','state-unit/s','state-unit**(1-b)/s','state-unit**(1-b)/s'][i],'fitted','Coefficient of the corresponding ordered term in `dy`; jointly fitted, not a separately measured flow.') for i in range(6)]
  pa += [par('h',p[6],'state-unit','fitted','Fitted shift of the state-power argument.'),par('b',p[7],'-','fitted','Exponent in `Max(y-h,epsy)**b`.'),par('a',p[8],'-','fitted','Exponent of normalized first input.'),par('d',p[9],'-','fitted','Exponent of normalized second input in the input product.'),par('e',p[10],'-','fitted','Exponent of the separate second-input forcing.')];pa +=[floor,par('epsu',1e-6,'-','fixed','Small dimensionless floor chosen for normalized input powers so evaluation is defined for zero or negative inputs; not a fitted physical cutoff.')]
  family='Generalized power-law nonlinear state-space vector field with bilinear interactions in transformed variables.'
  interpret=f'The transformed state is `{z}`; the combined forcing transform is `{v}` and the separate second-input transform is `{g}`. The terms `c0`, `c1*{z}`, `c2*{v}` and `c3*{g}` provide drift, state feedback and input forcing. The products `c4*{v}*{z}` and `c5*{g}*{z}` change state feedback with the inputs. All are mathematical effects; no flow mechanism is established.'
  origin='Generalized powers were initially tried as an empirical alternative to square-root laws, not taken from system physics. Additive transformed forcing improved the affine baseline, but residual state dependence led to adding two transformed state-input interactions. Power exponents and state shift were then jointly fitted. The nearly zero fitted product exponent indicates that this input dependence is weak and not separately well determined.'
  extra=' The fitted offset reaches its imposed upper bound and the product exponent is nearly zero; their interpretations are weakly identifiable. Below the offset the floor changes the law to a flat extension.'
 elif mode=='exp':
  z='sqrt(Max(y-h,epsy))';v='exp(a*(x1/n1-1))';expr=f'c0 + c1*{z} + c2*{v} + c3*(x2/n2) + c4*{v}*{z}'
  pa += [par('c'+str(i),p[i],'state-unit/s' if i in [0,2,3] else 'state-unit**0.5/s','fitted','Coefficient of the corresponding ordered drift, state feedback, exponential forcing, second-input forcing or mixed term; jointly estimated.') for i in range(5)]+[par('h',p[5],'state-unit','fitted','State shift before square root.'),par('a',p[6],'-','fitted','Exponential sensitivity to the normalized first input.'),floor]
  family='Exponential-input, square-root-state nonlinear state-space vector field.'
  interpret=f'`c0` is drift, `c1*{z}` is shifted square-root state feedback, `c2*{v}` is exponential first-input forcing, `c3*(x2/n2)` is linear second-input forcing and `c4*{v}*{z}` is an input-dependent state-feedback gain. The square root is a mathematical concave response; no tank interpretation is claimed.'
  origin='An exponential input map was tested as a smooth alternative to a high fitted power of the first input. State-input residual dependence motivated an exponential-by-square-root interaction. Linear second-input forcing was selected as a parsimonious starting structure, since richer fits gave an exponent near one.'
  extra=' Exponential input growth makes large first-input extrapolation particularly unsafe. The state offset reaches its upper bound and is not a physically established threshold.'
 else:
  z='sqrt(Max(y-h,epsy))';v='tanh(a*(x1/n1-u0))';g='tanh(b*(x2/n2-w0))';expr=f'c0 + c1*{z} + c2*{v} + c3*{g} + c4*{v}*{z} + c5*{g}*{z}'
  pa += [par('c'+str(i),p[i],'state-unit/s' if i in [0,2,3] else 'state-unit**0.5/s','fitted','Coefficient of the corresponding ordered drift, square-root feedback, two saturating forcings or two mixed terms; jointly estimated.') for i in range(6)]+[par('h',p[6],'state-unit','fitted','State shift before square root.'),par('a',p[7],'-','fitted','First-input hyperbolic-tangent slope.'),par('u0',p[8],'-','fitted','First-input normalized center.'),par('b',p[9],'-','fitted','Second-input hyperbolic-tangent slope.'),par('w0',p[10],'-','fitted','Second-input normalized center.'),floor]
  family='Saturating hyperbolic-tangent nonlinear state-space vector field with transformed bilinear feedback.'
  interpret=f'The saturating first- and second-input maps are `{v}` and `{g}`. Their separate forcings are `c2*{v}` and `c3*{g}`; mixed terms `c4*{v}*{z}` and `c5*{g}*{z}` modify the square-root state feedback. `c0` is constant drift. Saturation is mathematical boundedness, not a measured actuator property.'
  origin='Hyperbolic-tangent maps were a deliberately bounded alternative to power and exponential input extrapolation. A purely additive sigmoid model was inadequate, so shifted square-root state response and both transformed state-input products were added based on interaction residuals. No neural-network package or genetic symbolic search was used.'
  extra=' The second-input tangent is nearly linear on the observed domain; its slope, center and amplitude are strongly confounded. The finite iteration budget was reached, so this candidate is not asserted to be a converged global optimum. Bounded input maps do not by themselves guarantee stable state feedback.'
 model(label,expr,pa,nm(q),'nonlinear_dynamic.py',family,interpret,origin,limit+extra)
order=['CubicPolynomial','RationalGain','PowerFeedback','ExponentialFeedback','SaturatingFeedback']
json.dump({'order':order,'recommended':'CubicPolynomial','reason':'The complete cubic polynomial had the best validation free-running error among the derivative-fitted polynomial screens, approximately 0.0365 state-unit, without the singularity risks seen in higher degrees. Final output-error refitting on both files gives approximately 0.0233 state-unit training and 0.0334 state-unit validation RMSE, better than the other retained final fits. Those final figures are fitting diagnostics, not independent validation. Its interactions are explicit and it avoids fitted fractional-power branch thresholds. RationalGain is a lower-complexity alternative. Test performance remains unknown.'},open('models/pool.json','w'),indent=2)
